{
  format: 1
  name: set-declaration-rejects-foreign-member
  suite: static
  spec: ["#state-model", "§2.5", "§5.5", "§5.10"]
  note:
    '''
    §2.5: "Unknown members in a declaration object are invalid unless that declaration
    explicitly accepts application-defined member names."

    §5.5 defines the set declaration as `$set` and its element shape — "the value of
    `$set` is the shape of every member" — and gives it no other member and no
    application-defined member names. A set node carries an element type and nothing
    else: there is no place in it for a check, a default, or a normalization.

    So a `$check` written INSIDE a `$set` declaration is an unknown member of a closed
    declaration object, and §2.5 makes the definition invalid. That is the outcome this
    case pins, and the reason it matters is that the alternative is silent: an
    implementation that builds the set from `$set` and ignores what sits beside it loads
    a package whose declared membership constraint does not exist and is never enforced
    — no diagnostic at load, no rejection at admission, the constraint simply gone.

    The supported spelling is not in doubt. §5.10 puts a constraint over a row's values
    on the CONTAINING shape's `$check`, which is evaluated over the complete row and can
    therefore see the set through `.flags`:

      flags: { $set: "text" }
      $check: [["size(.flags) <= 3", "at most three flags"]]

    The rejected form here is the same intent written one level too deep. Both positions
    the defect reaches are covered: `docs.flags` is a set inside a keyed row, and `tags`
    is a set declared directly at the model root (§8.2).
    '''

  package: {
    $liasse: 1
    $app: "t.setmember@1.0.0"
    $model: {
      docs: {
        $key: "id"
        id: "text"
        // Invalid: `$check` is not a member of a `$set` declaration (§2.5, §5.5).
        // The membership constraint belongs on `docs`'s own `$check` (§5.10).
        flags: { $set: "text", $check: ["size(.flags) <= 3", "at most three flags"] }
      }
      // The same mistake at the model root, where a set is a root singleton member.
      tags: { $set: "text", $check: ["size(.tags) <= 5", "at most five tags"] }
    }
  }

  expect: {
    outcome: invalid
    violates: ["#state-model", "§2.5", "§5.5"]
    detail: "`$check` is an unknown member of a `$set` declaration; a membership constraint belongs on the containing shape's `$check` (§5.10)"
  }
}
